---
import Button from '../../components/Button.astro';
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { sitePath } from '../../lib/siteUrl';
---

<Layout title="Photo Retouching: Clone, Heal, Dodge and Burn" description="Persistent clone, heal, spot heal and patch repairs, a linear-light dodge and burn brush, frequency separation, and re-editable liquify. Local to your machine.">
  <div class="container-custom">
    <div class="feature-page">
      <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href={sitePath('/features')}>Features</a> / Retouching
      </nav>

      <h1 class="site-page-title">Repair, retone, and reshape raster images.</h1>
      <p class="feature-intro">
        Raster repairs that stay honest about what they are: persistent clone/heal
        tools with explicit sources, a linear-light dodge and burn brush, an exact
        two-band tone/texture split, and a brush-driven deformation stored as
        editable state rather than baked pixels.
      </p>

      <section class="feature-section">
        <h2>Clone, Heal, Spot Heal, and Patch</h2>
        <p>
          Every repair writes real document pixels on the layer you name, so strokes
          survive redraw, undo/redo, save/reopen, and export. Clone Stamp samples a
          frozen source anchor (aligned or unaligned), Healing Brush takes texture
          from the source and colour from the destination, Spot Heal picks a bounded
          nearby patch deterministically, and Patch copies a frozen source rectangle
          with a feathered edge.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>Strokes clip to the active selection — including Spot Heal and Patch — and alpha lock keeps repairs from spreading onto transparent pixels.</li>
          <li>Sampling scope is explicit: the layer, the layers below, or all visible raster layers. Deposits always land on the destination layer alone.</li>
          <li>Pen pressure modulates flow for clone and heal strokes; the mouse deposit is unchanged.</li>
          <li>Dodge and Burn apply exposure in stops in linear light under the brush, with a shadows/midtones/highlights focus — the same mathematics as the global Exposure control, applied locally. They adjust existing pixels only and never invent coverage.</li>
          <li>Zero-strength strokes, cancels, and repairs with no effect leave no history step behind.</li>
        </ul>
        <p><a href={sitePath('/docs/tools/retouching')}>Read the step-by-step retouching guide →</a></p>
      </section>

      <section class="feature-section">
        <h2>Frequency Separation</h2>
        <p>
          Split a raster layer into a tone/colour layer and a texture layer, retouch each
          independently, and let Varve recombine them. The split is exact by construction
          for flat content and within one 8-bit step per channel elsewhere — the dialog
          shows the measured reconstruction error before you commit.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>Radius is Gaussian sigma in layer pixels: zoom, preview scale, and export scale never change it.</li>
          <li>Tone and texture are ordinary layers, so clone, heal, paint, masks, and layer effects all work on either band.</li>
          <li>Change the radius later and Varve re-splits the current retouched state — your edits are not discarded.</li>
          <li>Flatten bakes the recombined result into one layer whenever you want to move on.</li>
        </ul>
        <p><a href={sitePath('/docs/tools/retouching')}>Read the step-by-step retouching guide →</a></p>
      </section>

      <section class="feature-section">
        <h2>Liquify</h2>
        <p>
          Push, expand, contract, twirl, restore, and smooth raster content with a brush.
          The deformation is stored as a bounded displacement field on the layer, so every
          stroke stays re-editable, reversible, and exportable without accumulating
          resampling blur.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>Freeze and thaw protection is available on raster targets; shared separation deformation keeps its unsupported group-level freeze state explicit.</li>
          <li>Pressure controls size and strength; push follows the pointer without drifting when you hold still.</li>
          <li>Deform one component of a frequency separation, or the recombined composite so tone and texture stay aligned.</li>
          <li>Reset, undo, and re-entry recover committed deformation instead of restarting from a flattened screenshot.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="feature-section">
        <h2>Built for real texture, not just portraits</h2>
        <p>
          The same tools clean product shots, even out fabric and material texture, rescue
          scanned artwork and paint surfaces, and correct local tone. There is no face
          detection, no automatic smoothing, and no universal "correct" radius: you judge
          the texture scale on your own content.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="feature-section note">
        <h2>No hidden AI, no hidden cost</h2>
        <p>
          These workflows run entirely on your machine with no model download, cloud
          inference, or network access. Clone and heal are exact copies and
          mean-shift adaptations of pixels that already exist — Spot Heal says so in
          the interface instead of borrowing a "content-aware" label — and dodge and
          burn work in Varve's 8-bit working representation. The precision contract
          is stated in the app rather than implied. Freeze protection on a shared
          separation deformation is not available in this version, and geometry-level
          vector liquify is a separate future capability — the interface says so
          rather than silently rasterizing your vectors or text.
        </p>
      </section>

      <div class="feature-cta">
        <h2>Shape the pixels without giving them up</h2>
        <Button href={sitePath('/download')}>Download Varve</Button>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .feature-page { max-width: 800px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 3rem 0; }
  .breadcrumb { font-size: 0.875rem; margin-bottom: 1.5rem; }
  .breadcrumb a { color: var(--text-secondary); }.feature-page h1 { font-family: var(--font-editorial); margin-bottom: 1rem; color: var(--text-primary); }
  .feature-intro { font-size: 1.25rem; color: var(--text-secondary); margin-bottom: 3rem; }
  .feature-section { margin-bottom: 3rem; }
  .feature-section h2 { font-size: 1.5rem; margin-bottom: 1rem; color: var(--text-primary); }
  .feature-section p, .feature-section li { color: var(--text-secondary); line-height: 1.7; }
  .feature-section li + li { margin-top: 0.65rem; }
  .note { border-left: 3px solid var(--accent-primary); padding-left: 1rem; }
  .feature-cta { border-top: 1px solid var(--border-default); padding-top: 2rem; }
  .feature-cta h2 { color: var(--text-primary); margin-bottom: 1rem; }
</style>
